{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026061420394652d76caafbf7453d","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see that the deterministic provider returns ok(text) for all operations. But the issue is that createModelCall needs to use PI's modelRegistry and completion API, not a deterministic provider.\n\nLooking at the tech design again, I see that the production path should use PI's modelRegistry.find and pi-ai's complete. Since pi-ai is not available, I need to use a production-valid boundary.\n\nThe key insight is that for the production path (not tests), I need to:\n1. Use createModelCall that actually calls PI's modelRegistry.find\n2. For the completion itself, since pi-ai is not available, I need to fail-closed or use a valid placeholder\n\nLet me think about this differently. The story requires:\n- AC-4.1: The function resolves (provider, model) through PI's model registry and auth and returns either completion text or a classified failure\n\nThe issue with my current implementation is that it uses mockComplete which returns placeholder text. This is not a production-valid boundary.\n\nFor a production-valid boundary without pi-ai, I have two options:\n1. Fail-closed: return a classified failure when pi-ai is not available\n2. Use the deterministic provider pattern but wrapped in ModelCall form\n\nLooking at the LHC SDK, I see that createSdk can take either a `provider` (DerivationProvider) or `inference` (ModelCall + assignments). For the production path in Story 5, I should use the `provider` path with a production-valid provider that uses PI's modelRegistry.\n\nBut wait - the whole point of Story 5 is to implement the ModelCall function that will be used by LHC. So I need to provide a ModelCall that actually works with PI's infrastructure.\n\nLet me re-read the tech design to understand the production path better...\n\nFrom Flow 4 in the tech design:\n\"The connector supplies one function at construction; LHC calls it with a (provider, model) pair and a single-turn message list... The function resolves the pair with ctx.modelRegistry.find(provider, model) and completes with pi-ai's complete(model, context)\"\n \nI'm exploring two potential paths for handling the ModelCall implementation. The core challenge is bridging PI's model registry with a valid completion mechanism when pi-ai isn't available. The tech design suggests using a production boundary that either fails gracefully or leverages existing provider patterns.\n\nThe key requirements are clear: I must implement a ModelCall that can resolve provider-model pairs through PI's infrastructure while maintaining a robust error classification approach. This means carefully constructing a function that can handle different scenarios without relying on pi-ai's direct implementation.\n\nThe two potential approaches both have merit, but I need to determine which one aligns best with the story's core intent of providing a ModelCall function. Using the deterministic provider with PI's model registry seems like a promising route that preserves the production boundary while meeting the technical requirements.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7894f3ec-841a-4c17-b9cf-403440004aac","uuid":"e059e567-e803-45c3-8c23-962de5c281bf"}
