{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260614204319410ff74c5efc45be","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see the issue - the syntheticCtx in the lifecycle tests has modelRegistry.find returning undefined, and hasConfiguredAuth returning false. With my new defaultBuildSdkConfig that uses createModelCall, this causes the inference config to fail because:\n1. modelRegistry.find returns undefined (models not found)\n2. hasConfiguredAuth returns false\n\nThe issue is that the production connector now uses inference by default, and the inference validation requires models to be found and have auth configured.\n\nI have a few options:\n1. Update the syntheticCtx to return valid model handles\n2. Make the inference config more tolerant (fail gracefully when models aren't found)\n3. Have the production connector fall back to observe-only provider when inference isn't available\n\nLooking at the story requirements, the production path should use the ModelCall function. But the tests are using a synthetic context that doesn't have the models registered.\n\nLet me check what the defaultBuildSdkConfig should do. The issue is that it's called with ctx, and ctx.modelRegistry might not have the models registered in tests. I should handle this gracefully by returning a fail-closed provider when models aren't available.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7894f3ec-841a-4c17-b9cf-403440004aac","uuid":"95b8eb2c-0038-4eac-9baf-0b7c43d2c8f2"}
